------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NETWORK USE — AGPL-3.0 SECTION 13
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

METIS is offered over a network. Section 13 therefore obliges any modified version to
offer its remote users the Corresponding Source of the running version.

This deployment satisfies that obligation through:

  * `GET /source` and `GET /api/source` on the API server — an unauthenticated JSON
    document naming the outbound licence, the repository, and the exact commit this
    process was built from, with direct links to that commit's tree and source
    archive.
  * A persistent footer in the web application, on every page including the sign-in
    page, linking to the source of the running deployment.

The commit is read at runtime from `METIS_SOURCE_COMMIT` (falling back to
`GIT_COMMIT`, then `SOURCE_COMMIT`). A deployment that leaves all three unset still
offers the repository, but cannot name the running commit — so **set
`METIS_SOURCE_COMMIT` at build or deploy time.** If you modify METIS and deploy it,
point `METIS_SOURCE_REPOSITORY_URL` at the repository that holds *your* modified
source; the offer must lead to your version, not to ours.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

METIS depends on third-party packages resolved by pnpm. They are not redistributed in
this repository — `pnpm-lock.yaml` names them exactly and each carries its own licence
in its own package. `pnpm licenses list --prod` reproduces the full list.

Four entries need a recorded decision rather than a line in a list.

1. jszip — dual-licensed `(MIT OR GPL-3.0-or-later)`
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A dual licence is an election, not an ambiguity, and leaving it unmade is the
   defect. **Zylos Labs LLC elects the MIT arm of jszip.** Both arms are compatible
   with an AGPL-3.0-only outbound licence, so the election costs nothing; it is
   recorded because an unrecorded election has to be re-derived by every reader, and
   because the GPL-3.0-or-later arm would otherwise be a live question about whether
   part of the tree is under a copyleft we did not choose.

2. @img/sharp-libvips-* — LGPL-3.0-or-later
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Platform-specific prebuilt libvips binaries pulled in by `sharp`. Compatible with
   AGPL-3.0: the LGPL permits relicensing under the corresponding GPL/AGPL version.
   The dynamic-linking question that a permissive outbound licence would have raised
   does not arise under a copyleft core.

3. @github/copilot — proprietary, "GitHub Copilot CLI License"
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Not declared in any METIS manifest. It arrives as a transitive dependency of
   `@github/copilot-sdk` (MIT), which is declared in `server/` and
   `server/copilot-svc/`, and it is present in the *production* dependency tree.
   `pnpm licenses list --prod` reports it as `Unknown` because its manifest says
   `SEE LICENSE IN LICENSE.md`; the actual terms are proprietary.

   It does not conflict with releasing METIS under AGPL-3.0. Its section 2 says in
   terms that "nothing in this License restricts your choice of license for your
   application or service, including distribution under an open source license", and
   its redistribution conditions are met by how METIS uses it: it is installed
   unmodified from npm as a dependency of an application with material functionality
   of its own, never repackaged or shipped standalone, and its own licence file
   travels with it inside its package.

   Two obligations follow for anyone redistributing a METIS *bundle* that includes
   resolved `node_modules` (a container image, for example) rather than source alone:
   the package must stay unmodified, and its `LICENSE.md` and copyright notices must
   be retained. Distributing METIS as source does not trigger either, because the
   package is not in this repository.

4. buffers@0.1.1 — no declared licence
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A transitive dependency five levels down (`exceljs` -> `unzipper` -> `binary` ->
   `buffers`) whose manifest declares no licence and which ships no licence file. It
   is not a conflict with AGPL-3.0 — it is an absence of a grant, which is a
   pre-existing risk that predates this licence choice and is not resolved by it.
   Recorded here so it is not mistaken for a cleared item. Tracked separately.

Verified for this release: the production dependency tree (1,129 packages) and the
full tree including development dependencies (1,460 packages) contain **no
`GPL-2.0-only` dependency**, which would have been genuinely incompatible with
AGPL-3.0. They contain no AGPL, SSPL or BSL dependency either. This was checked, not
assumed; re-check it when the lockfile changes.
